  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1201 PM MDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nQuiet weather and warm conditions are expected this afternoon.\nTomorrow, the thermal ridge will shift east over our area, \nincreasing 700mb temperatures around 4 to 6 degrees C. This, and \nplenty of sunshine, will put surface temps in the mid 90s to \naround 104 degrees across the plains Sunday afternoon. A Heat \nAdvisory will go into effect Sunday at noon for the I-25 corridor \nand Weld County. \n\nThe temperatures on Monday are a bit trickier. The upper level \nthermal ridge could remain over the area on Monday, keeping \ntemperatures well above normal for most of the area. However, \nthere is an upper level trough that's forecast to develop and move\nthrough the northern Great Plains and Canada on Monday. This will\ndevelop a surface low to our north, which will drag a cold front \nthrough our area sometime Monday. Models are trending earlier \nwith the cold front, now moving it into our northern counties \nsometime late Monday morning into Monday afternoon, which could \ndefinitely impact our highs on Monday, especially for our northern\nplains counties. The earlier front timing could also impact the \nnorthern extent of the Heat Advisory on Monday as well, but for \nnow we will leave the Advisory as is until we have higher \nconfidence in the timing. The NBM currently has a 25th to 75th \npercentile spread in max temperatures of around 10 degrees \n(mid/upper 80s to upper 90s) for areas along the CO/WY border, due\nto that uncertainty with the front timing. High temperatures \nsouth of a Boulder to Yuma line, should be less impacted by the \nfront given the current frontal timing, so we are more confident \nin those highs reaching into the upper 90s to low 100s Monday \nafternoon. But we'll have to keep an eye on that front as we get \ncloser. \n\nOn Tuesday and Wednesday, that upper level high is expected to push \nback westward. This combined with the surface front will give us a \nbrief reprieve from the above normal temperatures. The exact amount \nof cooling for Tuesday is still uncertain, with ensemble members\nranging anywhere from the mid 60s to upper 90s. Most likely highs\nlook to be in the low 80s for the plains on Tuesday. On \nWednesday, temperatures will warm back closer to normal (around 90\ndegrees). The best chance for precip this week will be \nalong/behind the front and Tuesday and Wednesday afternoons as the\nstronger subsidence moves off to the west and easterly surface \nwinds allow for some weak upslope. However, our \"best chances\" are\nstill only around 20% to 40%, as our moisture struggles to \nrecover. Temperatures will gradually warm for Thursday and Friday \nas the thermal ridge inches closer to the area each afternoon.",
